WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.580
Portfolio organization affects how quickly a reviewer can understand your work.

2
00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:14.040
A project may be technically good, but if the repository is confusing, full of unrelated files, or missing a README, the reviewer may not find the evidence.

3
00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:16.620
Good organization is not decoration.

4
00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:19.120
It is part of professional communication.

5
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:22.440
Every project should have a clear README.

6
00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:31.100
The README should explain the purpose of the project, the target skill area, the safe environment used, the structure of the repository, and how to read the artifacts.

7
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:35.280
If setup steps are safe to include, document them.

8
00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:39.620
If the project is a writeup rather than runnable code, say that.

9
00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:44.360
If screenshots or diagrams are sanitized, say so.

10
00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:47.100
The README is the front door to the project.

11
00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:49.300
Use folders intentionally.

12
00:00:49.400 --> 00:01:00.200
A simple structure might include docs, diagrams, screenshots, sample-data, queries, scripts, reports, or tests, depending on the project.

13
00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:02.380
Do not dump everything at the top level.

14
00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:09.680
Do not include generated clutter, private notes, tool caches, or files that are not part of the evidence.

15
00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:13.220
If an artifact is included, it should support the project story.

16
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:16.540
Screenshots and diagrams should be named clearly.

17
00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:23.040
A file called dashboard-before.png is more useful than image1.png.

18
00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:28.580
A diagram should include enough labels to be understandable without exposing private details.

19
00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:31.960
Sample data should be clearly fake or public.

20
00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:42.140
Configuration examples should not contain real credentials, real tokens, private keys, internal hostnames, or private addresses when those details are sensitive.

21
00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:45.220
Status labels help reviewers interpret the work.

22
00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:51.160
You can mark projects as complete, draft, archived, in progress, or learning exercise.

23
00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:55.020
This is especially useful if your portfolio includes experiments.

24
00:01:55.120 --> 00:02:01.680
It is fine to show growth, but do not make a reviewer guess whether an unfinished project is meant to represent your best work.

25
00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:06.980
A small number of polished projects is better than many confusing or abandoned ones.

26
00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:09.420
Consistency matters over time.

27
00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:15.840
Use similar README sections, naming patterns, redaction habits, and license choices where appropriate.

28
00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:18.980
If a project uses references, cite them.

29
00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:23.080
If a project includes code, document assumptions and limitations.

30
00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:27.260
If a project is no longer representative, archive it or remove it.

31
00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:31.600
Organization makes your portfolio easier to review and easier for you to maintain.
